6. Log Transformation

A log transformation replaces a value with its logarithm.

\[
X'=\log(X)
\]

It is particularly useful for right-skewed data.

Example

Income data may contain:

20,000
25,000
30,000
40,000
50,000
5,00,000

The very large value can create strong right skewness.

A log transformation compresses large values more strongly than small values.

Python

import numpy as np

df["log_salary"] = np.log(df["Salary"])

If zero values exist

Normal logarithm of zero is undefined. A common practical transformation is:

df["log_salary"] = np.log1p(df["Salary"])

log1p(x) calculates:

\[
\log(1+x)
\]

Important Point

Log transformation is not appropriate for arbitrary data containing negative values without first choosing an appropriate transformation strategy.


---

7. Box-Cox Transformation

Box-Cox transformation is a statistical transformation used to reduce skewness and make positive-valued data more suitable for statistical modeling.

It generally requires strictly positive values.

Python

from scipy.stats import boxcox

transformed, lam = boxcox(df["Salary"])

Here:

transformed = transformed data

lam = estimated transformation parameter


Why use Box-Cox?

Reduce skewness.

Stabilize variance in some situations.

Make data closer to a normal distribution.


Important

Box-Cox requires positive values.


