Part G — Inferential Statistics

23. What is Inferential Statistics?

Inferential statistics uses information from a sample to make conclusions about a larger population.

Example

Suppose a college has 10,000 students.

It may be difficult to survey all 10,000 students.

Instead, we select 200 students and analyze their data.

Population
    ↓
Select Sample
    ↓
Analyze Sample
    ↓
Make Inference
    ↓
Population


---

24. Population vs Sample

Population

The population is the complete group being studied.

Example:

> All students in a college.



Sample

A sample is a smaller group selected from the population.

Example:

> 100 students selected from that college.



Population	Sample

Entire group	Subset
Usually larger	Usually smaller
Population parameter	Sample statistic
Example: all students	Example: 100 students


Symbols

Population mean:

\[
\mu
\]

Sample mean:

\[
\bar{x}
\]


---

25. Why is Inferential Statistics Used?

It helps us:

Estimate population parameters.

Test hypotheses.

Make predictions.

Make decisions using sample data.

Reduce the time and cost of collecting data.



