Part H — Sampling Techniques

26. Convenience Sampling

In convenience sampling, participants are selected because they are easy to access.

Example

Asking students sitting near you to complete a survey.

Advantages

Simple.

Fast.

Cheap.


Disadvantages

May be biased.

May not represent the population accurately.



---

27. Systematic Sampling

In systematic sampling, every \(k\)-th member is selected after an appropriate starting point.

Example

Select every 10th student:

10th
20th
30th
40th
...

Advantage

Simple and systematic.

Disadvantage

Can be problematic if the population has a pattern that aligns with the sampling interval.


---

28. Simple Random Sampling

In simple random sampling, each member has an equal chance of being selected.

Pandas Example

sample = df.sample(
    n=10,
    random_state=42
)

Here:

n=10 → selects 10 rows.

random_state=42 → makes the random selection reproducible.



---

29. Stratified Sampling

In stratified sampling, the population is divided into meaningful groups called strata, and samples are selected from each group.

Example

College
   ↓
-----------------
Engineering
Management
Science
-----------------
   ↓
Sample from each group

Advantage

Ensures important subgroups are represented.


---

30. Cluster Sampling

In cluster sampling, the population is divided into clusters, and entire clusters are selected.

Example

All colleges in Maharashtra
          ↓
Select 5 colleges
          ↓
Survey students in selected colleges

Stratified vs Cluster

Stratified	Cluster

Sample from every important group	Select some groups
Groups are called strata	Groups are called clusters
Goal is subgroup representation	Goal is efficient sampling



---

31. Sampling in Pandas

Random sample

sample = df.sample(n=10)

20% sample

sample = df.sample(frac=0.2)

frac=0.2 means approximately 20% of rows.


