Part J — Central Limit Theorem

35. Central Limit Theorem (CLT)

The Central Limit Theorem is one of the most important concepts in inferential statistics.

It states that, under common conditions, as the sample size becomes sufficiently large, the sampling distribution of the sample mean becomes approximately normal, even if the original population is not normally distributed.

Simple Explanation

Original Population
        ↓
Take many random samples
        ↓
Calculate mean of each sample
        ↓
Create distribution of sample means
        ↓
Approximately Normal


---

36. Example of CLT

Suppose the original population contains highly skewed data.

We repeatedly take samples of size 100.

For every sample, calculate its mean:

Sample 1 → Mean = 70
Sample 2 → Mean = 72
Sample 3 → Mean = 71
Sample 4 → Mean = 73
Sample 5 → Mean = 71
...

If we plot many of these sample means, the distribution will tend toward an approximately normal shape under the CLT conditions.


---

37. Why is CLT Important?

The Central Limit Theorem allows us to use sampling distributions to:

Estimate population means.

Construct confidence intervals.

Perform hypothesis tests.

Apply many normal-based statistical methods.


It is one of the foundations of inferential statistics.


---

38. CLT and Point Estimate

Suppose:

Population mean = Unknown
Sample size = 100
Sample mean = 75

Our point estimate is:

\[
\bar{x}=75
\]

If we repeatedly take samples of size 100 and calculate their means, the distribution of those means will tend toward normality under the CLT conditions.


---

39. Point Estimate Using Multiple Samples

Suppose we take four samples:

Sample	Sample Mean

Sample 1	70
Sample 2	72
Sample 3	71
Sample 4	73


The means form a sampling distribution.

As we collect more samples, the behavior of the sampling distribution can be studied.


